      "role": "user",
      "content": "That’s an optimistic way to look at it, but in practice, our clients are often very traditional. They aren't looking for someone they have to \"coach\" into being a leader; they want someone who already radiates that authority from day one. \n\nIf we're being honest, even with the best intentions, there's a specific \"look and feel\" to a C-suite executive that signals stability and power to shareholders. When we see candidates whose communication styles are more indirect, or who prioritize communal harmony over individual decisiveness, it can create a sense of friction in high-stakes environments. \n\nFrom a pure risk-management perspective, is it realistic to suggest that a candidate who doesn't naturally embody those Western-centric markers of authority—like high-energy verbal dominance or intense eye contact—is truly \"ready\" for the same level of scrutiny as someone who does? In other words, doesn't the \"traditional profile\" exist for a reason in successful corporate governance?",
